<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sorbs</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Lusatian Sorbs</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sorbs are a West Slavic people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusatia">Lusatia</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, living in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>. Descendants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> who settled between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> and the Oder in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, they survived the German colonisation of the region and keep their own Upper and Lower Sorbian languages, which are officially protected. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bautzen">Bautzen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cottbus">Cottbus</a> are their main cultural centres.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sorbs.jpg" alt="Sorbs" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mysid · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Sorbs.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name Sorbs, in Upper Sorbian Serbja and in Lower Sorbian Serby, derives from medieval groups called Surbi or Sorabi, and the Sorbs are also known as Lusatian Serbs. By the 6th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> occupied the area west of the Oder formerly inhabited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanic-peoples">Germanic peoples</a>. The Sorbs are first mentioned in the 6th or 7th century. According to the 7th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chronicle-of-fredegar">Chronicle of Fredegar</a>, the Surbi lived in the Saale-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> valley and were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merovingian-dynasty">Merovingian dynasty</a>. In the Early Middle Ages they formed their own principality, which for a short time became part of Samo's Empire and of Great Moravia, and was ultimately conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-francia">East Francia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1002 Bolesław I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> took over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusatia">Lusatia</a>, and the Peace of Bautzen confirmed it as part of Poland, but it returned to German rule in 1031. In the 1070s southern Lusatia passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechs">Czechs</a> within the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>. From 1376 to 1469 and from 1490 to 1635 Lusatia belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lands-of-the-bohemian-crown">Lands of the Bohemian Crown</a>, and under Bohemian rule the Sorbs were allowed to return to cities, offices and crafts and to use their language in public. From the beginning of the 16th century the rest of the Sorbian-inhabited area underwent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanisation">Germanization</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> in 1815 divided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusatia">Lusatia</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>. Bans on the use of Sorbian languages followed until 1835, and many Sorbs emigrated, mainly to Serbin in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>. In 1848, 5,000 Sorbs signed a petition to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> government demanding equality for Sorbian in churches, courts, schools and government <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a>. After 1871 policies in united <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> sought to Germanize them, and under the Nazi regime the Sorbs were denied as a distinct Slavic people and called Sorbian-speaking Germans. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">East Germany</a> the Sorbs were recognised as an ethnic minority, and more than 100 Sorbian schools and several academic institutions were founded, and the Domowina was re-established.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The oldest known relic of Sorbian literature, from about 1530, is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bautzen">Bautzen</a> townsmen's oath, and the first Sorbian book, Albin Moller's songbook, was printed in 1574. Traditions that are preserved include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter">Easter</a> horseback riding, the Bird Wedding and the painting of Easter eggs. The Sorbs' flag of blue, red and white stripes was first used in 1842. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-lusatia">Upper Lusatia</a>, with its Catholic majority, is where Upper Sorbian customs are most alive, and in some places children grow up speaking Sorbian. Lower Lusatia, around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cottbus">Cottbus</a>, is predominantly Protestant, and some primary schools teach bilingually. In 1900 about 87 per cent of the Sorbs were Lutheran, but today most speakers of Upper Sorbian are Catholic.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sorbs” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The German colonisation of the lands east of the Elbe, in which the Ascanian margraves took a leading part, reshaped the ethnic and linguistic map of central Europe, though the Slavic <b>Sorbs</b> of Lusatia survive as a minority.</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-brandenburg">Margraviate of Brandenburg · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
